  "notes": "USAGE:\nVery common in sports reporting, business news, and political commentary. Implies that one is fighting hard but at a disadvantage. Often collocates with する (to struggle) or を{強|し}いられる (to be forced into a struggle).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {苦戦|くせん}する: to struggle, to have a hard fight\n- {苦戦|くせん}を{強|し}いられる: to be forced into a tough battle\n- {苦戦|くせん}が{予想|よそう}される: a tough fight is expected\n- {苦戦|くせん}が{続|つづ}く: the tough fight continues\n- {苦戦|くせん}の{末|すえ}: after a hard-fought battle\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {善戦|ぜんせん}: a good fight — implies fighting well despite losing or being the underdog\n- {奮闘|ふんとう}: spirited fight, hard struggle — emphasizes the effort and determination",
